Smoke is EMERGENT aggregation, rendered as soft puffs. Set up an emitter into a universe with inter-quantum collisions + an aggregation ladder, then render every frame as a white puff:
  universe.activeAxes = { x:true, y:true, z:true };   // z spread → volumetric
  universe.brownianMotion = true;
  universe.withInterQuantumCollisions = true;
  [4,8,12].forEach(n => universe.addDimension(n));     // voxel-size aggregation ladder
  // emitter: a small source, modest burst, longish wavelength
  // render: white billow on ALL frames
  renderDefaults: { smokeLayerMin: 0, smokeColor: [238,238,242], smoke: 14 }
The billow (bigger puffs where frames parented into bigger voxels) EMERGES from the collisions — there is no billow code. Copy setupFxSmoke. Higher 'smoke' = bigger puff scale.

A projectile is a MOVING LargeObject: an occluder rect that advances each tick via a tick-fn, with a collision op-program that pushes quanta out of its way. Copy the Bullet sugar:
  var b = new Bullet(universe, { x: muzzleX, y: cy, w: 80, h: 80, speed: 0.5,
                                 reposition: 'closest3d', billboard: true, color:[235,205,150] });
Internals: the LO is registered with collision_type NONE + actions:['BOUNCE'] + reposition (so actionCompiler emits an eject op-program — NOT a native bounce). A pushed tick-fn advances BOTH the JS wrapper rect (renderer reads it) AND the engine rect (universe.updateObjectProperty). The compression bow-wave in front of it is FREE physics — no wake code. billboard:true makes the flat plane face the camera (a disc, not an edge-on sliver) for "coming at the viewer."

Push a tick function onto universe.experimentTickFunctions in your setup factory — the SAME contract the Blackbody thermostat and the Bullet advance use. Example emission ramp (full→0 over ticks 180→600):
  var t = 0, full = burstSize;
  universe.experimentTickFunctions.push(function () {
    t++; if (t < 180) return;
    var frac = Math.max(0, 1 - (t - 180) / (600 - 180));
    emitter.setBurstSize(Math.round(full * frac));
  });
Do NOT invent a new framework hook (e.g. an onEachTick option) for this — the tick-fn IS the per-tick hook. Continuous control belongs in the setup factory's tick-fn; one-shot beats belong in the director's schedule(atTick,fn).

setup factory:
  universe.activeAxes = { x:true, y:true, z:true };
  universe.brownianMotion = true;
  universe.withInterQuantumCollisions = true;
  [4,8,12].forEach(n => universe.addDimension(n));
  var emitter = new QuantumEmitter(universe, {
    source_rects: [[cx, cy, 1, 1]], burst_size: 20,
    fundamentalWaveLength: 64, emit_velocity: 1, charge: 'Negative', auto_fire: true
  });
  return { emitter };
page renderDefaults:
  { smokeLayerMin: 0, smokeColor: [238,238,242], smoke: 14, glowIntensity: 0 }
That's it — the billow emerges from the collisions. To recolor or scale the billow, change smokeColor / smoke. To make it a puff-burst, ramp burst_size to 0 after N ticks via a tick-fn.

Use runner.cameraControl — the camera sugar handle from the active renderer's getCameraControl(). It is null in 2D (orthographic camera, nothing to orbit) and a handle object in 3D (three.js OrbitControls). Do NOT sniff the renderer type. Pattern:
  var cam = runner.cameraControl;
  if (cam) { cam.setPreset('down-barrel'); }
Presets typically include 'down-barrel', 'table-top', 'rooftop' (renderer-defined). To enable 3D in the first place: (1) set universe.activeAxes = { x:true, y:true, z:true } in your setup factory; (2) set window._egpt3D = true on the experiment page — render-boot switches to PerspectiveCamera. Capability-check is mandatory — never write `if (renderer instanceof ThreeProvider)`. A renderer that does not implement getCameraControl() returns null and the camera widget is simply absent, with zero breakage.
